<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gytheio</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gythion, Gytheion</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gytheio is a port town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Laconia</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, on the Laconian Gulf at the entrance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mani">Mani</a> peninsula. In antiquity it was the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>. The Frankish castle of Passavant (Passava) was built nearby in the 13th century, and in the 19th century Gytheio became the main harbour of the Mani.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gytheio.jpg" alt="Gytheio" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diliff · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gythio_Port,_Greece_-_Diliff.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Gytheio lies at the northeastern corner of the Mani Peninsula, on the northwestern end of the Laconian Gulf, about 40 km southeast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> and linked to it by Greek National Road 39. It was built on a hill called Koumaros or Laryssio near the mouth of a usually dry river nicknamed Xerias. Offshore lies the small island of Cranae, where myth says <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and Helen spent their first night together before leaving for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a>; it now has a causeway to the mainland. The town is the seat of the municipality of East Mani.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Myth names <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castor-and-pollux">Castor and Pollux</a>, as founders of ancient Gythium. Phoenician traders from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> may have used it for a purple dye trade based on the gulf's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrian-purple">murex</a>. In classical times it was a community of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perioeci">perioikoi</a>, dependent on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>. The Athenian admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tolmides">Tolmides</a> burned it in 455 BCE with 50 ships and 4,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoplite">hoplites</a>, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-peloponnesian-war">First Peloponnesian War</a>. It was rebuilt and probably became the shipyard of the Spartan fleet; in 407 BCE Alcibiades saw thirty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trireme">triremes</a> being built there. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epaminondas">Epaminondas</a> took it in 370 BCE after three days, and the Spartans recaptured it three days later. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-macedon">Philip V of Macedon</a> failed to take it in 219 BCE.</p><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabis">Nabis</a>, Gythium was a major naval arsenal and port, and it fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> only after a long siege. It then became the leading community of the Union of Free Laconians, a league of twenty-four, later eighteen, communities declared free by Augustus. It exported purple dye, porphyry and rose antique marble, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> built a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> that survives. The city was destroyed in the 4th century, whether by Alaric and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a>, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> or by the earthquake of 375, which is not recorded. It remained a small village through Byzantine and Ottoman times. It grew after Tzannetos Grigorakis built his tower on Cranae, and during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-war-of-independence">Greek War of Independence</a> refugees made it a major town.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Gytheio opened a modern port in the 1960s, with ferries to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kythira">Kythira</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, and is the seat of the Orthodox Diocese of Gytheion and Oitylo. The ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> and acropolis were found by Dimitris Skias in 1891. Most ruins of ancient Gythium lie under the gulf, and the Dimitrios shipwreck rests in shallow water by Valtaki beach. Cranae's Tzannetakis Tower is now the Historical and Cultural Museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mani">Mani</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gytheio” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 1220 John of Neuilly, hereditary marshal of the Principality of Achaea, received a fief at the entrance to the Mani peninsula and built a castle there called Passavant, now Passava, near <b>Gytheio</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/barony-of-passavant">Barony of Passavant</a></li></ul></section>
